==Finnish==

===Etymology===
Probably from {{etyl|gmq-osw|fi}} {{m|gmq-osw|siel}}, {{m|gmq-osw|siäl}} ("soul") , compare modern Swedish {{m|sv|själ}}, German {{m|de|Seele}} and English {{m|en|soul}}.
Alternatively, borrowed from {{etyl|bat|fi}} *sḗjlā (compare Lithuanian {{m|lt|siela}}), along with other Finnish loanwords from Baltic languages of the same thematic group, see etymology sections of e.g.taivas (“heaven”), Jumala (“God”).

